# ==============================================================================================
# Environment template for the extracted Catalog service.
#
# This is a COMMITTED TEMPLATE. It declares the NAME of every environment variable the service
# reads and deliberately carries NO VALUES.
#
# ⛔ NOTHING IN THIS SUBTREE LOADS A `.env` FILE, AND THAT IS WORTH READING BEFORE YOU COPY THIS ONE.
# There is no `dotenv` dependency and no loader of any kind: `src/config/env.ts` reads
# `process.env` and nothing else. Creating a `.env` file therefore changes NOTHING on its own —
# the variables have to be present in the process environment of whatever runs the service. Two
# ways to do that, and this template is written to suit both:
#
#   1. A LOCAL SHELL, where the values are turned into environment variables for one command.
#
#      ⛔ NEVER MAKE A SHELL READ THE FILLED-IN COPY AS A PROGRAM. Earlier guidance here told you to
#      copy this template and then load it with the shell's source builtin under its allexport
#      option. That instruction was unsafe for two independent reasons (review finding
#      SEC-DOC-ENV-01, CWE-78), and the commands are described here rather than reproduced, so that
#      nothing in this file can be copied and run by mistake:
#
#        (a) SOURCING EXECUTES THE VALUES. The source builtin runs a file as a shell PROGRAM, so a
#            value is not data — it is code. A password containing a command substitution, a
#            backtick, a semicolon, `&&` or a pipe is EXECUTED rather than assigned, and a
#            high-entropy password is exactly the kind of string that contains such characters. The
#            old note defended itself by observing that every line here is a plain assignment
#            containing none of the characters a shell treats specially. That was true of THIS
#            TEMPLATE, which holds no values, and irrelevant to the artifact the very next line told
#            you to load: the copy you had just filled with secrets. A safety property of the
#            template was being claimed on behalf of a file that does not exist yet and will not
#            share it.
#        (b) THE ALLEXPORT RESTORE WAS CONDITIONAL. The command chained three steps with `&&`, so
#            turning allexport back OFF depended on the loading step SUCCEEDING. An absent file, an
#            unreadable one or any non-zero result skipped the restore and stranded the shell with
#            allexport still on, silently exporting every variable assigned for the rest of that
#            session.
#
#      Use a parser that treats the file as DATA instead:
#
#          node --env-file=.env …
#
#      `--env-file` is built into the Node 20 line this service targets and is the simplest correct
#      option: Node reads and parses the file itself, so no shell ever interprets the contents, a
#      value containing shell syntax is assigned verbatim rather than run, and the calling shell is
#      left completely unmodified. For a non-Node consumer, the requirement generalises — parse the
#      file, never execute it.
#
#      ⚠️ NAME THE LOCAL COPY EXACTLY `.env` AND NOTHING ELSE. .gitignore lists that literal
#      filename rather than a wildcard — deliberately, so this template stays committable — which
#      means `.env` is ignored but `.env.local`, `.env.dev` and `.env.production` are NOT, and a
#      copy under any of those names is a credential file git will happily stage. Verify with
#      `git check-ignore -q .env && echo ignored` before writing a secret into anything.
#
#   2. A DEPLOYMENT, where the file is not used at run time at all. Lambda function configuration,
#      a task definition, a Kubernetes secret or a CI variable group each set the process
#      environment directly; this template is then the CHECKLIST of names to populate there, which
#      is its main job. Infrastructure as code is out of scope for this deliverable (AAP 0.2.2.5),
#      so no such artifact is authored here.
#
# The copy is ignored and this template is not: .gitignore lists the exact filename `.env` rather
# than a wildcard, so a filled-in file can never be committed while the names stay under version
# control. No credential is hardcoded anywhere in this port and none is committed here.
#
# Nothing below is needed to build, type-check, lint or test this subtree. src/config/env.ts
# validates the connection facts without opening a connection, the Google integration is a stub
# that makes no network call, and the suites are unit tests. A value is required only where a real
# MySQL connection is actually opened.
#
# ⛔ BEFORE YOU DEPLOY ANYTHING CONFIGURED BY THIS FILE — RUNTIME LIFECYCLE GATE (review finding
# SEC-RUNTIME-01, CWE-1104). This service targets Node.js 20 / the Lambda `nodejs20.x` managed
# runtime, and that line is OUT OF SUPPORT. Two separate authorities, two separate facts, one shared
# date — stated separately so each can be checked against the body that owns it:
#
#   - Node.js 20 reached upstream end-of-life on 2026-04-30, per the Node.js Release working group's
#     schedule (github.com/nodejs/Release), whose 20.x row ends in that date.
#   - AWS deprecated the Lambda `nodejs20.x` managed runtime on 2026-04-30, per the AWS Lambda
#     supported-runtimes table. The date matches the line above because AWS aligns managed-runtime
#     deprecation to upstream EOL — a consequence of that policy, not one fact double-counted.
#
# Security patching has stopped. Already-deployed functions remain invocable on every published
# timeline — the later gates are control-plane gates on creating and updating a function, not on
# invoking one.
#
# The later control-plane gates, from the AWS runtimes table as read on 2026-08-03: creating a new
# function on nodejs20.x is blocked from 2027-02-01, and updating an existing one from 2027-03-03.
# Those are the current dates. They supersede two earlier published schedules, and the two are
# superseded for DIFFERENT reasons, which matters to anyone re-checking them:
#   - 2026-06-01 / 2026-07-01 — the earliest AWS bulletin. Elapsed, and observably without effect.
#   - 2026-08-31 / 2026-09-30 — the standard 30-day/60-day cadence. NOT yet elapsed; as of the read
#     date above both are still ahead. What retires this pair is AWS's current table stating the
#     later 2027 dates, not the calendar, so check it against the table and do not treat the arrival
#     of 2026-08-31 as either confirmation or refutation.
# AWS states it is delaying these dates for some runtimes and that its own tables are the only
# authority, so both earlier pairs are obsolete revisions of a moving forecast rather than live
# alternatives. tsconfig.json carries the full dated account and the mechanism behind the revisions;
# this is a pointer to it.
#
# The pin is DELIBERATE and cited, not an oversight: AAP 0.5.5 resolves this exact evidence with
# "The pin stands.", and AAP 0.5.3.2 rejects a newer @types/node on the ground that definitions
# must track the runtime. Uplifting is a product decision recorded against the plan.
#
# So filling this file in is NOT by itself clearance to ship to production. `npm run build`
# announces the same gate on every run, and build/esbuild.mjs lists the version couplings a bump has
# to move together. Read one of those before deploying.
#
# WHERE THESE VARIABLES ARE READ
# ------------------------------
# In exactly one place. src/config/env.ts is the sole reader of the process environment in this
# subtree. Configuration then flows one way only, but it flows in THREE SEPARATE BRANCHES rather
# than down one chain — the branches share a reader, not a path, and each ends at a different
# consumer:
#
#   1. THE NINE CONNECTION FACTS reach the driver, and nothing else reads them:
#
#     environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/database.ts -> the module-scope mysql2 pool
#                    reads + validates    builds the pool options   injected into the adapters
#
#   2. GOOGLE_FEED_HOST reaches the feed's entry point THROUGH THE COMPOSITION ROOT, which is the
#      detail an earlier revision of this diagram got wrong by drawing it as a direct edge. The
#      handler does not import src/config/env.ts at all: the container exposes the validated
#      configuration and the feed's wiring reads `container.config.googleFeed` from it, which is why
#      the handler is substitutable in a test without touching the environment:
#     environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/handlers/googleFeedHandler.ts
#                    reads (presence)     renders the feed on the configured host, unjudged
#
#     environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/container.ts -> src/handlers/googleFeedHandler.ts
#                    reads + validates    exposes config.googleFeed   renders on the validated host
#
#   3. THE THREE SETTING_* NAMES reach the setting adapter. They are the setting values whose LEGACY
#      default is computed at run time rather than stored, so no frozen table can hold them and the
#      deployment supplies what the excluded services would have returned:
#
#     environment -> src/config/env.ts -> src/config/container.ts -> StaticSettingResolver
#                    reads + validates    passes straight through     resolves 3 of 18 names
#
# Only branch 1 passes through src/config/database.ts. Reading the three as one chain would suggest
# the pool sees the feed host and the setting values, which it never does.
#
# Every layer below the config layer — domain, ports, adapters, services, validation and
# integrations — receives what it needs by constructor injection and never reaches for the
# environment itself. The list below is therefore exhaustive by construction: it is exactly the
# key set src/config/env.ts reads, no more and no less — NINETEEN names, of which SIX are
# REQUIRED and THIRTEEN are OPTIONAL:
#
#     loader                       total  required                       optional
#     ---------------------------  -----  -----------------------------  ------------------------------
#     loadDatabaseConfig()             9  DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_NAME,  5  DB_TLS_MODE,               4
#                                         DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD           DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT,
#                                                                        DB_QUEUE_LIMIT,
#                                                                        DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS
#     loadGoogleFeedConfig()           1  GOOGLE_FEED_HOST            1  —                          0
#     loadSettingsConfig()             3  —                           0  the 3 SETTING_* names      3
#     loadResourceBoundsConfig()       6  —                           0  the 6 CATALOG_* bounds     6
#     ---------------------------  -----  -----------------------------  ------------------------------
#     TOTAL                           19                              6                            13
#
# The six REQUIRED names are DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD and GOOGLE_FEED_HOST.
# The thirteen OPTIONAL ones are DB_TLS_MODE, DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT, DB_QUEUE_LIMIT,
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS, the three SETTING_* values and the six CATALOG_* bounds.
#
# THIS SPLIT IS NOT MAINTAINED BY HAND ON EITHER SIDE. src/config/env.ts declares the inventory as
# data — ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE_CENSUS — and asserts its own arithmetic when the module loads; the
# suite additionally reads that census, compares it against every process.env access in the loader's
# source, and compares it against THIS file. So a name added on one side and not the other fails a
# test rather than being discovered by a reader. Earlier revisions of this header said "SIXTEEN
# names / TEN optional" and, before that, "TEN required and THREE optional"; both are gone, and the
# mechanism above is what replaced the practice of correcting them by hand.
#
# EVERY OPTIONAL NAME IN THIS FILE IS COMMENTED OUT, and every required one is present with an empty
# value. That is not cosmetic: src/config/env.ts refuses a blank optional value, so a template whose
# optional names were present-but-empty would fail to load the moment it was copied and sourced,
# instead of applying the absent-behaviour documented beside each one. The required names are left
# present and empty because they have to be filled in, and a name you must supply is more useful
# visible than commented.
#
# ⚠️ REQUIRED HERE MEANS EXACTLY ONE THING: PRESENT, WITH NO DEFAULT SUBSTITUTED. Absence of any of
# the six is fatal at load, with an error naming the variable. The thirteen optional names are NOT
# lenient — a name that is present but BLANK is still refused — they simply have a defined
# behaviour when ABSENT, and that behaviour differs per name rather than being one rule:
#
#     DB_TLS_MODE             absent -> the verified transport mode; only a loopback DB_HOST may
#                             lower it, which is the loader's rule and not a concession
#     DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT     absent -> the option is OMITTED from the pool entirely, so the
#     DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS   driver's own documented behaviour governs; this port states no figure
#     DB_QUEUE_LIMIT          absent -> the lowest permitted bound, 1, because the driver reads an
#                             unset queue limit as UNLIMITED and selecting unlimited silently is the
#                             one thing DECISION C exists to prevent
#     the three SETTING_*     absent -> that ONE setting name raises a classified configuration
#                             error when resolved, and every other name keeps working
#     the six CATALOG_*       absent -> the route that needs the bound REFUSES, with an error naming
#                             the variable to set. Loading succeeds with all six absent — which is
#                             what keeps the build, the linter and the suite runnable with no
#                             environment — but an absent bound is never read as "unbounded". See
#                             RESOURCE BOUNDS below for which route reads which name.
#
# A name declared here that nothing reads, and a name read there that is missing here, are both
# defects, and the census cases in the suite are what report them.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# PROVENANCE — the legacy application recorded only four connection-related values in source
# ==============================================================================================
# The legacy CFML application never held connection details in source. It named a
# ColdFusion/Railo datasource and let the application server resolve it out of band. The
# datasource struct has exactly three members and the bootstrap republishes those plus the ORM
# dialect:
#
#   config/configApplication.cfm:L2  `<cfset this.datasource.name = "Slatwall" />`
#   Application.cfc:L78              publishes `datasource`         <- this.datasource.name
#   Application.cfc:L81              publishes `datasourceUsername` <- this.datasource.username
#   Application.cfc:L84              publishes `datasourcePassword` <- this.datasource.password
#   Application.cfc:L87              publishes `databaseType`       <- this.ormSettings.dialect
#   org/Hibachi/Hibachi.cfc:L12-L13  set username and password to the EMPTY STRING
#
# Those four values are the entire derivation basis for the names below, and because the
# credentials are set to `""` there is no legacy credential to carry across — which is why every
# value below is blank rather than seeded.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# DECISION — the three-way runtime dialect probe is collapsed to a fixed MySQL target
# ==============================================================================================
# config/configORM.cfm:L3-L14 chose the persistence dialect at RUNTIME: L3 probed the server with
# `<cfdbinfo ... type="Version">`, then three branches inspected the reported product name, with
# no else-branch, so an unrecognised product left the dialect unset.
#
# This port targets MySQL only. The Sw* schema is retained unchanged and read and written through
# `mysql2`, so the branch is resolved at DESIGN time rather than at configuration time.
#
# The consequence is stated so it cannot be mistaken for an oversight: there is deliberately no
# DB_DIALECT and no DATABASE_TYPE variable here, and none is read anywhere in src/**. Offering one
# would advertise a portability this port does not implement. No database engine version is
# asserted either, because the legacy repository pins none.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# DOCUMENTED ABSENCES — host, port and schema name are not declared in legacy source
# ==============================================================================================
# "Slatwall" at config/configApplication.cfm:L2 is a ColdFusion/Railo DATASOURCE NAME: an alias
# registered in the application server's administrator, which the server resolves out of band to
# a JDBC URL, a schema, a user and a password. It is NOT a schema name, NOT a host, NOT a port and
# NOT a user, so it is never used as one; it appears in this file only in comments, with its
# locator. admin/views/main/nodatasource.cfm:L12 confirms the indirection by instructing an
# operator to define the datasource name and nothing else.
#
# DB_HOST, DB_PORT and DB_NAME are therefore new here by necessity, and they are left BLANK rather
# than guessed. Nothing below is defaulted — not the schema name, not the host, and not the port.
# Supplying a plausible-looking value would assert a fact the source does not state.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# TRANSPORT AND RESOURCE BOUNDS — declared as NAMES, never as values
# ==============================================================================================
# FIVE VARIABLES ARE REQUIRED. FOUR MORE ARE OPTIONAL. THAT SPLIT IS THE BOOT CONTRACT, AND IT IS
# WORTH READING BEFORE THE LIST.
#
# Required, with no default of any kind: DB_HOST, DB_PORT, DB_NAME, DB_USER, DB_PASSWORD. These
# are the five AAP 0.4.1.3 documents. A connection target and an identity cannot be guessed, so
# the loader refuses to start without them and names the one it is missing.
#
# Optional, each with a fallback stated below and in src/config/env.ts: DB_TLS_MODE,
# DB_QUEUE_LIMIT, DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT, DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS. An earlier revision REQUIRED all
# four, on the reasoning that these are the settings whose absence the driver reads as a decision
# — with no transport setting it negotiates no encryption, and it reads a queue limit of zero as
# "no limit". That reasoning is sound and is preserved; what was wrong was the remedy. Requiring
# them meant a deployment configured exactly to the plan's documented five-variable contract
# FAILED CLOSED at cold start, so the code and the document an operator reads first disagreed.
#
# The remedy is a safe fallback rather than a mandatory value, so absence never selects the poor
# arrangement AND never blocks the boot:
#
#   DB_TLS_MODE            absent -> verified. Absence ENCRYPTS. Cleartext must be asked for by
#                          name, and is still refused for any host that is not a loopback literal.
#   DB_QUEUE_LIMIT         absent -> 1, the floor this loader already enforces for a supplied
#                          value. This is the one option that cannot be left to the driver, whose
#                          own default of zero MEANS unbounded. The fallback is a floor, not a
#                          capacity estimate.
#   DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT    absent -> the option is OMITTED and the driver's own bounded default
#   DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS  applies. Omitting is what lets this port state no figure at all, which
#                          is what AAP 0.4.1.3 requires when it records that "pool sizing is not
#                          carried over because the legacy application delegates pooling to the
#                          CF/Railo server and pins nothing in source" (IR-12).
#
# So: no value is committed in this file, no figure is authored in any source file, and the only
# number the loader itself names is that floor of 1. src/config/env.ts holds the full reasoning
# as its DECISION E, and src/config/database.ts as its DECISION C and DECISION I.
#
# Still deliberately absent, because for these the driver's own behaviour genuinely defers a
# choice rather than making a poor one: no idle timeout, no maximum-idle count, no keep-alive
# interval, no statement bound, no reset-on-release setting and no connection-lifetime value.
# And no ceiling is validated for any of the three numbers, because a ceiling would be a
# capacity figure with no source. The two timing values the legacy source does declare are
# request-scoped controls that belong with the execution-model mismatches in the README, not
# with connection configuration.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# VARIABLES — names only; no VALUE is committed anywhere in this file
# ==============================================================================================
# Listed in the order src/config/env.ts validates them, which is the order in which a missing or
# malformed value is reported.
#
# TWO SHAPES APPEAR BELOW, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE REQUIRED/OPTIONAL SPLIT RATHER THAN A STYLE:
#
#   NAME=            a REQUIRED name, present with an empty value. You must fill it in; leaving it
#                    empty fails the load, naming the variable (except DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD,
#                    where an empty string is a real legacy value — see their entry).
#   # NAME=         an OPTIONAL name, COMMENTED OUT. Absent selects the fallback documented beside
#                    it; a bare `NAME=` does NOT, because src/config/env.ts refuses a blank optional
#                    value. This file is meant to be copied and sourced as-is, so an optional name
#                    left active-and-empty would break the copy — review finding F5.
#
# The single exception to "no value is committed" is `# DB_TLS_MODE=verified`, which is commented out
# and carries the fallback the loader already applies, so uncommenting it changes nothing until the
# value is changed. No number, host, credential or identifier appears anywhere in this file.

# Connection target. REQUIRED, both of them. Not declared anywhere in legacy source — see
# DOCUMENTED ABSENCES above.
#
# DB_HOST accepts a BARE HOST and nothing else. The accepted grammar, enforced at module load by
# src/config/env.ts and reported with a message that names it:
#
#   - a registered name or IPv4 literal, as defined by RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 — ASCII only, so an
#     internationalised name is supplied as its A-label (punycode);
#   - an IPv6 address, written either bracketed (`[::1]`) or bare (`::1`). The bare form is outside
#     RFC 3986 but is a legitimate host for this driver, and `::1` is the loopback literal the
#     transport rule below exists to serve;
#   - an IPvFuture literal.
#
# Refused, each with a message that says why: a scheme prefix such as `mysql://`, a
# `user:password@` prefix, a `:` and port — the port is DB_PORT and belongs there — a path, a
# query, a fragment, whitespace including a trailing newline, a control character, any non-ASCII
# character, and a FILESYSTEM SOCKET PATH. The last one is worth stating: a unix socket is a
# legitimate way to reach MySQL, but it is configured through the driver's own socket option and
# src/config/database.ts sets none — it passes this value as the host, where a path would be
# resolved as a hostname and fail. A path here could never have connected, so it is refused at
# load rather than accepted and left to fail later. The same grammar is applied to
# GOOGLE_FEED_HOST below, which is checked for presence only and accepts a port suffix as it accepts
# everything else.
#
# DB_PORT must be a plain base-ten TCP port number between 1 and 65535.
DB_HOST=
DB_PORT=

# Schema holding the existing Sw* tables. REQUIRED. This service reads and writes that schema and
# neither creates nor migrates it. Must be non-blank before a connection is opened. NOT the datasource
# name from config/configApplication.cfm:L2 — see DOCUMENTED ABSENCES above.
#
# Length is bounded at 64 characters, MySQL's documented maximum for a database identifier. A longer
# value cannot name a schema on any server, so it is refused at load — naming this variable — rather
# than deferred to the first query, where the driver would report an unknown-database error that names
# neither the variable nor the environment. The bound is the server's, not a capacity figure chosen
# here. No character screening is applied: MySQL permits a wide range in a quoted identifier and the
# schema is the fixed contract both systems share, so refusing a name that genuinely exists would be
# the worse failure.
DB_NAME=

# Credentials, REQUIRED, mapped from `this.datasource.username` and `this.datasource.password`
# (Application.cfc:L81, Application.cfc:L84). NO value is committed here.
#
# These two are the only variables that may legitimately stay empty at run time: the validator
# requires them to be PRESENT but permits an empty string, which reproduces the legacy `""` at
# org/Hibachi/Hibachi.cfc:L12-L13 exactly. Empty and absent are therefore different states, and
# only absent is an error.
#
# That tolerance exists for source parity and is NOT a recommended production posture. In any real
# deployment supply an explicit account scoped to least privilege on the Sw* schema, unless the
# database is intentionally and securely configured for credentialless access.
DB_USER=
DB_PASSWORD=

# How the connection is protected in transit. OPTIONAL — when unset the mode is `verified`, which
# is the fail-safe direction: absence encrypts, and the unencrypted mode has to be asked for by
# name. Exactly one of two tokens, matched case-sensitively:
#
#   verified   TLS is required, the server's certificate chain is verified against the runtime's
#              trust store, and the certificate is checked against the host connected to. The
#              only accepted value for any host that is not a loopback literal.
#   disabled   No TLS. ACCEPTED ONLY when DB_HOST is a loopback literal — `localhost`, an address
#              in 127.0.0.0/8, or the IPv6 loopback. Its purpose is the local development
#              database, whose self-signed certificate no public trust store can verify. Setting
#              it for a remote host is refused at load, not warned about.
#
# There is deliberately no third token, and none that keeps TLS while skipping verification: an
# unverified session is indistinguishable from an intercepted one.
#
# A private certificate authority needs no variable here and no code change. Supply the root to
# the Node runtime through its own documented extra-CA mechanism and keep this set to `verified`;
# src/config/database.ts reads no certificate file and parses no certificate.
#
# COMMENTED OUT, LIKE EVERY OPTIONAL NAME IN THIS FILE. Absent and blank are different states here and
# only absent selects the documented fallback: src/config/env.ts REFUSES a blank value, because a name
# typed and left empty would look like working configuration while behaving as though nothing had been
# supplied. A bare `DB_TLS_MODE=` in a copied file therefore fails the load — which is what review
# finding F5 recorded, since this template is meant to be `cp`-ed and sourced. Uncomment it only to
# ASK for `disabled` against a loopback database; leave it commented to get `verified`.
# DB_TLS_MODE=verified

# Resource bounds for the pool. ALL THREE ARE OPTIONAL. A value that IS supplied must be a plain
# base-ten integer of at least one; none has a ceiling, because a ceiling would be a capacity
# figure with no source. Blank is a misconfiguration rather than a way to say "unset".
#
# DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT is the greatest number of connections the pool may open. UNSET -> the option
# is omitted and the driver's own bounded default applies. AAP 0.3.2 records that a single
# connection is the shape usually recommended for a serverless caller, but records it as
# engineering rationale only and never as a figure — so the number is the operator's to state, and
# not this port's to assert even as a fallback.
#
# DB_QUEUE_LIMIT is the greatest number of connection requests that may wait once the connection
# limit is reached; beyond it a request fails fast instead of queueing without bound. UNSET -> 1,
# the floor above. This is the ONE bound that is not delegated to the driver, and the reason is
# the substance of the setting: the driver reads zero as "no limit" AND zero is its default, so
# omitting the option would select the unbounded queue this value exists to prevent. ZERO IS
# REJECTED when supplied, for the same reason.
#
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS is how long the driver may spend establishing a connection before it
# fails. UNSET -> the option is omitted and the driver's own documented connect timeout applies.
# It bounds connection setup only; it is not a statement, request or invocation timeout, and it is
# not a latency target of any kind.
#
# ALL THREE ARE COMMENTED OUT, and none carries an example value. Absent is the correct way to say
# "this deployment states no bound" — src/config/env.ts refuses a blank one, so a bare
# `DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=` in a copied file fails the load rather than falling back (review finding F5).
# No number is written here even as an illustration, because AAP 0.3.2 records the serverless pool shape
# as engineering rationale only and never as a figure: a committed example would be this port asserting a
# capacity value the source never states (IR-12). A deployment that has MEASURED its own capacity
# uncomments the name and states its own number.
# DB_CONNECTION_LIMIT=
# DB_QUEUE_LIMIT=
# DB_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS=

# ==============================================================================================
# GOOGLE PRODUCT FEED — the one name that is not a connection fact
# ==============================================================================================
# The host authority every absolute URL in the Google merchant feed is built on: the canonical
# replacement for the legacy `CGI.HTTP_HOST` reads at
# integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm:L14, :L15, :L22, :L23 and :L24.
#
# WHY IT IS CONFIGURATION AND NOT A REQUEST HEADER. The legacy took the value straight from the
# request, which on any platform means the caller chooses it. Three routes open as a result:
# markup in the header lands inside element text nodes and can add or replace feed fields; every
# product, image and additional-image URL in the document is rebased onto whatever authority the
# caller names; and one bare ampersand leaves the document with no defined XML parse at all.
# src/config/env.ts holds the full reasoning as its DECISION F.
#
# FORM. A bare host authority — `store.example.com`, `store.example.com:8080`, an IPv4 literal,
# or a bracketed IPv6 literal — optionally followed by `:` and a port. NO scheme: the `http://`
# prefix is hardcoded in the builder because the legacy hardcodes it.
#
# WHAT IS ENFORCED, AND WHERE. The value is judged in THREE places, none of which trusts the others
# to have run:
#
#   1. src/config/env.ts, at module load, requires it to be PRESENT and NON-BLANK and to match the
#      full RFC 3986 section 3.2.2 `host` production with section 3.2.3's optional `port`. A value
#      outside that production raises a ConfigurationError naming GOOGLE_FEED_HOST, so a
#      misconfiguration is a cold-start failure rather than a bad document.
#   2. src/handlers/googleFeedHandler.ts re-applies an authority-delimiter deny check when the feed's
#      collaborators are constructed, so a container built from a hand-supplied value is judged too.
#   3. src/integrations/google/ProductFeedBuilder.ts re-applies it per render, atomically, before any
#      byte is produced.
#
# THE GRAMMAR, EXACTLY. `host [ ":" port ]`, where `host` is one of:
#   - a registered name: ASCII letters, digits and `- . _ ~ ! $ & ' ( ) * + , ; =`, or a `%XX`
#     percent-encoded octet. This subsumes an IPv4 literal such as `203.0.113.7`.
#   - a bracketed IPv6 literal, e.g. `[2001:db8::1]`, which must also parse as a real IPv6 address.
#   - a bracketed IPvFuture literal, e.g. `[v1.fe80::a+en1]`.
# and `port`, when present, is held to exactly the rule DB_PORT is held to: a plain base-ten integer
# in 1..65535.
#
# WHAT IS REFUSED, BY NAME, AT LOAD. Each of these raises rather than reaching the feed:
#   - a scheme prefix, e.g. `http://store.example.com` or `https://store.example.com`. The scheme is
#     not yours to set here; see FORM above.
#   - userinfo, e.g. `user@store.example.com` or `user:pw@store.example.com`. `CGI.HTTP_HOST` could
#     never carry it — RFC 9110 section 7.2 excludes it from the Host field — and admitting it would
#     let `store.example.com@evil.example` move the origin of every URL in the document (CWE-601).
#   - a path, query or fragment: `store.example.com/feed`, `store.example.com?a=1`,
#     `store.example.com#top`. A `#` would otherwise collapse all five URLs onto one page.
#   - a backslash, whitespace anywhere (leading, trailing or embedded) and any ASCII control character.
#   - a bare, UNBRACKETED IPv6 address such as `2001:db8::1`. Bracket it.
#   - any non-ASCII character. Supply an internationalised name as its A-label (`xn--…`), which is the
#     form that reaches the wire regardless.
#   - a blank value. Blank is not a way to say "unset", and this name is required.
#
# THE DIAGNOSTIC NAMES THE VARIABLE AND NEVER ECHOES THE VALUE, because a rejected authority is
# attacker-supplied text by hypothesis.
#
# WHAT IS STILL NOT DECIDED ANYWHERE, so the remaining exposure reads as a decision:
#   - THE IDENTITY OF THE HOST. Nothing checks whether the authority you name is one you control.
#     There is no allowlist of permitted feed hosts and no DNS or reachability probe: an allowlist would
#     refuse authorities a conforming deployment may legitimately name, and neither list exists in the
#     legacy source to carry across.
#   - NORMALISATION. A value that passes is published VERBATIM. It is not trimmed, case-folded,
#     punycoded, bracketed, stripped of a default port or upgraded to a secure scheme.
#
# ⚠️ THE IMPORTER'S IMPORT-SOURCE POLICY IS A DIFFERENT THING, and the difference is worth naming
# because the two look alike. This value is a host the OPERATOR states once and the feed writes into
# output. The importer's location arrives from a CALLER and is dereferenced server-side, which is a
# different threat with a different owner: it is decided by ProductImportSourcePolicy, an injected
# collaborator declared in src/ports/repositories/ProductRepository.ts, and the composition root's
# default reader refuses fail-closed until a deployment supplies one.
#
# REQUIRED, and therefore present-and-blank here, like the five connection facts above and unlike the
# thirteen optional names, which are commented out. There is no host to inherit from legacy source, so
# no value is committed and none is defaulted (IR-12) — this is the sixth and last name you must fill
# in.
GOOGLE_FEED_HOST=

# ==============================================================================================
# SETTING VALUES — the three of eighteen whose legacy default is COMPUTED, not stored
# ==============================================================================================
# Three further names, all OPTIONAL, and one collaborator below the config layer reads them:
#
#   src/adapters/settings/StaticSettingResolver.ts   holds three run-time-computed setting values
#
# Its `StaticSettingResolverConfiguration` argument is optional and defaults to `{}`, so a deployment
# that states none of the three still resolves every setting name that comes from a frozen literal.
# The values are read HERE anyway, and not somewhere below, because src/config/env.ts is the sole
# reader of the environment in this subtree; src/config/env.ts holds the full reasoning as its
# DECISION G.
#
# ⛔ SEVEN NAMES WERE REMOVED FROM THIS FILE AND NONE OF THE SEVEN IS COMING BACK, so their absence
# reads as a decision rather than an omission. They were PRODUCT_IMPORT_ALLOWED_SCHEMES,
# PRODUCT_IMPORT_ALLOWED_HOSTS, PRODUCT_IMPORT_MAXIMUM_RESPONSE_BYTES,
# PRODUCT_IMPORT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS, PRODUCT_IMPORT_MAXIMUM_REDIRECTS, URL_TITLE_MAXIMUM_PROBES and
# SKU_MAXIMUM_COMBINATIONS_PER_PRODUCT.
#
# ⭐ WHAT THEY CONFIGURED IS NOT GONE; THE ENVIRONMENT NAMES ARE. Each of those seven made a deployment
# supply a figure or a list to src/config/env.ts before the service would load AT ALL, because loading is
# fail-fast: the value had to be invented by somebody before anything ran, and relocating an invention is
# not avoiding it (AAP 0.7.3 standard 9, IR-12). The controls themselves are INJECTED COLLABORATORS
# instead, wired in src/config/container.ts, so a deployment that has measured its own capacity states the
# figure where the figure is its own:
#
#   the SKU combination ceiling      SkuCombinationBudget, src/services/SkuService.ts
#   the URL-title probe ceiling      UrlTitleProbeBudget, src/util/urlTitle.ts
#   the import-source policy         ProductImportSourcePolicy, declared on
#                                    src/ports/repositories/ProductRepository.ts and enforced in
#                                    src/adapters/mysql/MySqlProductRepository.ts
#
# ⭐ AND TWO OF THE CEILINGS DO HAVE ENVIRONMENT NAMES AGAIN — DIFFERENT NAMES, IN A DIFFERENT SECTION,
# FOR A DIFFERENT REASON. A security review measured that no route carried an operator's figure into the
# graph at all, so every ceiling was unreachable. The six CATALOG_* names in FINITE RESOURCE BOUNDS below
# are the route that was missing, and that section states exactly which route reads which name and what an
# unset name produces. The distinction that keeps this decision intact is WHEN the figure is demanded: the
# seven removed names were demanded at module load, from every deployment, for behaviour some of them never
# reach; the six CATALOG_* names are demanded by the route that applies them, and only when it runs.
# SKU_MAXIMUM_COMBINATIONS_PER_PRODUCT and URL_TITLE_MAXIMUM_PROBES themselves do not return.
#
# ⛔ AND NO PRODUCT_IMPORT_* NAME RETURNS, ON ITS OWN GROUND. Three of the five were properties of a
# retrieval CLIENT — a byte cap, a timeout and a redirect count — and this subtree ships no HTTP client at
# all (mysql2 is its only runtime dependency), so there is nothing here for them to configure; the
# obligations they once carried are stated as obligations on an implementor of ProductImportSourceReader.
# The other two were an allow-list of schemes and of hosts, which a deployment supplies to the policy
# collaborator directly.
#
# What is deliberately NOT here, so those absences read as decisions too: no timeout, retry, chunk or
# queue figure for the importer's one-hour legacy request budget
# (model/service/ProductService.cfc:L65-L68), because that is execution-model mismatch M1 and the
# port FLAGS it rather than resolving it; no figure for the feed's 360-second render budget
# (integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm:L9), mismatch M2, for the same reason; and no
# ceiling on any number in this file, because a ceiling would be a capacity figure with no source.
#
# THESE THREE SETTING NAMES ARE OPTIONAL, and the optionality is a contract rather than leniency.
# They are three of the thirteen optional names in this file; the other ten are the four transport and
# pool values of DECISION E and the six CATALOG_* bounds, and the census near the top of this file
# tabulates every one of them with its absent-behaviour.
#
# What distinguishes these three is the REASON they are optional. Fifteen of the eighteen setting names
# this slice reads resolve from frozen literals and need nothing here; these three are computed at run
# time by the legacy setting service, two of them from services this slice excludes, so no static table
# can hold them.
#
# Supplying one makes the corresponding setting name resolvable. LEAVING ONE UNSET leaves that ONE
# name — and only that name — raising a classified configuration error, while every other name
# continues to work: a deployment that reads only `globalDateFormat` must not be forced to invent
# currency and fulfillment lists it has no business knowing.
#
# LEAVE THEM UNSET, NOT EMPTY. Absent and blank are different states and only absent is permitted
# here: a name typed and left empty is refused at load, because a blank path resolves to a bare
# suffix and a blank identifier list is a list of nothing, and both would look like working
# configuration while behaving as though nothing had been supplied. (Note the deliberate contrast
# with DB_USER and DB_PASSWORD, where blank is a real legacy value and absent is the error.)
#
# SETTING_APPLICATION_ROOT_MAPPING_PATH is the CFML application scope's `applicationRootMappingPath`,
# from which model/service/SettingService.cfc:L164 derives `globalAssetsImageFolderPath`. Supply THE
# PATH ONLY, without the `/custom/assets/images` suffix — the suffix is the legacy's and the setting
# adapter appends it, so a path that already includes it produces a doubled suffix.
#
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_CURRENCIES is the comma-delimited currency identifier list
# model/service/SettingService.cfc:L222 computes as `getCurrencyService().getAllActiveCurrencyIDList()`
# — for example `USD,CAD`. A delimited string rather than separate names because the legacy consumer
# reads it with list functions (model/entity/Sku.cfc:L373 and :L375), and the Currency* family is out
# of scope, so the deployment supplies the list that service would have returned.
#
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_FULFILLMENT_METHODS is the comma-delimited fulfillment-method identifier list
# model/service/SettingService.cfc:L223 computes as
# `getFulfillmentService().getAllActiveFulfillmentMethodIDList()`. Two independent facts make it
# unanswerable statically: the computation reaches the excluded Fulfillment* family, and the three
# seeded rows at config/dbdata/SlatwallSetting.xml.cfm:L14-L16 are scoped by productTypeID while the
# port's resolution context carries an entity kind and identifier only.
#
# All three are commented out rather than left as bare empty assignments, because for these three an
# empty assignment is an error while omission is correct.
# SETTING_APPLICATION_ROOT_MAPPING_PATH=
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_CURRENCIES=
# SETTING_SKU_ELIGIBLE_FULFILLMENT_METHODS=

# ==============================================================================================
# FINITE RESOURCE BOUNDS — six names, and the SERVICE DOES NOT SERVE WITHOUT THEM
# (review findings SEC-DOS-01, SEC-DOS-02, SEC-DOS-03; CWE-400)
# ==============================================================================================
# ⛔⛔ READ THIS BEFORE DEPLOYING. These six names were OPTIONAL, and "unset" meant "unbounded". A
# security review measured what that produced and reported three findings: unbounded SKU combination
# generation that could also fail to terminate (SEC-DOS-01), smart-list and feed work with no
# materialisation, complexity, image or byte ceiling on any authenticated route (SEC-DOS-02), and
# unbounded URL-title collision probing (SEC-DOS-03). Every one of the six is now APPLIED, and a
# deployment that states none of them has a service that REFUSES every request rather than one that
# runs unbounded. That is deliberate, and it is the direction the findings required.
#
# ⭐ NOTHING IS INVENTED, WHICH IS THE CONSTRAINT THAT SHAPES ALL SIX. No source file authors a figure
# and no default exists anywhere (AAP 0.7.3 standard 9, IR-12). Each bound is carried by a RESOLVER: an
# unstated figure produces a ConfigurationError that NAMES THE VARIABLE TO SET, from the route that
# needed it. So the refusal reports an absent decision; it never substitutes for one. Choosing the
# figures is the operator's job, because only the operator knows the catalog, the instance size and the
# invocation timeout they are deploying against.
#
# WHICH ROUTES NEED WHICH — so a partial configuration is diagnosable rather than mysterious:
#   CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY      every route (all reads go through the smart list)
#   CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY   every route (same)
#   CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST      sku.createSkus, and product.saveProduct through it
#   CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION   product.saveProduct, product.saveProductType,
#                                                 brand.saveBrand
#   CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD     google:feed.product
#   CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES        google:feed.product
#
# The anonymous feed route additionally refuses EARLY, before it composes a query or resolves an image
# path, and it names the first of its four figures that is unset. Every other route refuses at the
# moment the figure would have been applied.
#
# LEAVE AN UNUSED NAME UNSET, NOT EMPTY, like the three setting names above: a value that IS supplied
# must be a plain base-ten integer of at least one. Zero, a negative, a fraction, a blank and anything
# non-numeric are all refused at load, naming the variable. Zero is the dangerous one to admit
# silently — a bound of zero would refuse every request rather than bounding it. None has a ceiling,
# because a ceiling would be a capacity figure with no source.
#
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY is the largest number of records ONE smart-list query may
# materialise. Applied by both execution members of the query builder — including executeRecords,
# which is the member the feed reads through and whose statement carries no LIMIT — by counting before
# hydrating and REFUSING an over-budget selection rather than truncating it.
#
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY is the largest number of QUERY-COMPLEXITY units ONE
# compiled smart-list statement may carry, where a unit is one bound parameter plus one ORDER BY term
# plus one registered join. This ONE figure bounds keyword cardinality (keywords × keyword properties),
# FI:/FIR: list cardinality (one placeholder per element), OrderBy cardinality and join count together
# — and it bounds statement SIZE too, because no caller-supplied VALUE ever reaches the emitted text:
# every one is bound through a ? placeholder, so a megabyte-long keyword adds one unit and zero
# characters of SQL. Refused after composition and before execution.
#
# CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST is the largest number of SKU combinations ONE merchandise
# createSkus request may enumerate — the odometer at model/service/SkuService.cfc:L58-L211. A value of
# 1 is meaningful rather than degenerate: :L67 starts `totalCombos` at 1, so a ceiling of 1 admits the
# option-less default SKU and refuses everything larger. Applied between the count and the first SKU
# allocation, so an over-budget request constructs, attaches and validates nothing. This is a WRITE
# path, so no anonymous caller can reach it. Note that a combination product which is no longer an
# exact integer is refused REGARDLESS of this figure, because the legacy loop cannot terminate on such
# an input — that check needs no operator decision.
#
# CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION is the maximum number of uniqueness probes ONE URL-title
# derivation may issue. It bounds the PROBE rather than the algorithm: the slug transformation and the
# `-2`-first suffix sequence of model/service/DataService.cfc:L53-L71 are unchanged for every
# derivation that stays in budget. It counts the pre-loop probe at :L62 as well as the loop's, so a
# ceiling of 1 admits exactly the no-collision case. Also a WRITE path.
#
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD is the largest number of g:additional_image_link elements
# ONE feed record may emit. integrationServices/google/views/feed/product.cfm:L24 loops the product's
# whole image collection inside the per-SKU loop with no ceiling, and each image costs one image-path
# port call. Checked before the loop, so an over-budget record resolves no path at all.
#
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES is the largest size in BYTES the rendered feed document may
# reach. Bytes rather than characters because the response is UTF-8 on the wire, where one character
# can be four bytes. Measured on the finished document, and REFUSED rather than truncated — a
# truncated RSS document is malformed, not smaller.
#
# All six are commented out rather than left as bare empty assignments, because for these an empty
# assignment is an error while omission is correct — and omission now means the corresponding routes
# refuse.
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_RECORDS_PER_QUERY=
# CATALOG_SMART_LIST_MAX_PREDICATES_PER_QUERY=
# CATALOG_SKU_MAX_COMBINATIONS_PER_REQUEST=
# CATALOG_URL_TITLE_MAX_PROBES_PER_DERIVATION=
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_IMAGES_PER_RECORD=
# CATALOG_GOOGLE_FEED_MAX_RESPONSE_BYTES=
